AlgoTrade: SQLite -> PostgreSQL on AWS RDS
demo pair: data/demo/20261117_1405/trading_bot.db + Postgres database cloudsync_demo_20261117_1405; prepared run artifacts/demo/20261117-1405

==============================================================================
  3. Integrity constraints and the quarantine   (~3 min)
  Syllabus topic: CHECK, NOT NULL, FK; rejected by the database
==============================================================================
  SAY:
   - Postgres decides what's invalid, not Python (D11). Python only routes the failure.
   - A rejected row is kept in rejected_rows with the constraint it broke, so nothing is silently lost.

  > Insert an impossible candle (high 90, low 110) straight into Postgres
  PASS  rejected by Postgres: new row for relation "candles" violates check constraint "candles_ohlc_close_in_range"

  > What the bulk load quarantined (rejected_rows)
    source table  constraint            rows
    ------------  --------------------  ----
    delta_cache   delta_snapshots_pkey  2050
    delta_cache: 2,050 duplicate groups on the natural key. The highest source id wins (D10),
    the rest are kept here instead of being dropped.
  [Enter = next step]

  > Now the bot writes a bad candle (close above high) into SQLite, and we sync
    SQLite accepted close = 22878.45 for candle id 28148126 (high is 22828.45). SQLite has no CHECK here.
    $ cloudsync sync once --sqlite data/demo/20261117_1405/trading_bot.db --dsn "<demo dsn as sync_writer>"
    |   "upserts": 0,
    |   "deletes": 0,
    |   "rejected": 1,
    |   "paused_tables": [],
    |   "backlog": 0
    | }
    exit 0 in 1.0 s
  PASS  the cloud quarantined it: [('28148126', 'candles_ohlc_close_in_range')]
  [Enter = next step]

  > The bot corrects the row, and we sync again
    $ cloudsync sync once --sqlite data/demo/20261117_1405/trading_bot.db --dsn "<demo dsn as sync_writer>"
    |   "upserts": 1,
    |   "deletes": 0,
    |   "rejected": 0,
    |   "paused_tables": [],
    |   "backlog": 0
    | }
    exit 0 in 0.9 s
  PASS  corrected row left the quarantine and is back in the cloud: (datetime.datetime(2026, 3, 27, 15, 29), 22824.85, 22828.45, 22820.55, 22824.7)
  [Enter = next step]

==============================================================================
  6. Indexing and concurrency   (~3 min)
  Syllabus topic: B-tree / expression indexes, query plans, WAL, locking
==============================================================================
  SAY:
   - The bot asks for its latest 200 candles by IST time. The views expose ts AT TIME ZONE 'Asia/Kolkata',
   - so a plain index on ts can't be used. An expression index on that same expression can (D63).

  > EXPLAIN ANALYZE with the expression index
    with candles_ist_ts: Execution Time: 2.366 ms
      ->  Index Scan using instruments_symbol_key on instruments i (actual time=0.027..0.028 rows=1 loops=1)
      ->  Index Scan using candles_ist_ts on candles c (actual time=0.045..0.237 rows=375 loops=1)
      ->  Index Scan using candle_indicators_pkey on candle_indicators ci (actual time=0.003..0.003 rows=1 loops=375)

  > Same query with the index dropped, inside a transaction we roll back (DDL is transactional)
    without the index: Execution Time: 15.534 ms
      ->  Seq Scan on instruments i (actual time=0.012..0.013 rows=1 loops=1)
      ->  Seq Scan on candles c (actual time=12.224..13.507 rows=375 loops=1)
      ->  Index Scan using candle_indicators_pkey on candle_indicators ci (actual time=0.003..0.003 rows=1 loops=375)
    15.53 ms -> 2.37 ms. Index restored by the rollback: True
  [Enter = next step]
  SAY:
   - SQLite: in rollback-journal mode a reader blocks the writer. WAL lets the bot write while we read.

  > Live WAL vs rollback-journal benchmark (3 s per mode, on copies of the demo SQLite)
    $ cloudsync bench-wal --sqlite data/demo/20261117_1405/trading_bot.db --out artifacts/demo/20261117-1405/live/bench_wal --seconds 3
    exit 0 in 14.9 s
    | Journal mode | Writes OK | Write lock errors | Write p50 ms | p95 ms | p99 ms | max ms | Reads OK | Read lock errors | Read p50 ms |
    |---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
    | delete | 6 | 0 | 6.041 | 1308.744 | 1308.744 | 1308.744 | 3 | 0 | 1243.688 |
    | wal | 462 | 0 | 1.108 | 1.676 | 1.896 | 6.69 | 3 | 0 | 1349.146 |
    Recorded 10 s run (Day 2, artifacts/day2/wal_benchmark.md): 16 writes in delete mode vs 1,569 in WAL.
  [Enter = next step]

==============================================================================
  7. Point-in-time recovery on AWS RDS   (~2 min)
  Syllabus topic: backup and recovery, WAL archiving, PITR
==============================================================================
  SAY:
   - RDS keeps snapshots plus the write-ahead log, so it can rebuild the database at any second.
   - We fingerprinted one trading day, deleted it, and restored RDS to a moment before the delete.
   - A restore takes 10-20 minutes, so this is the recorded run from Day 5.

  > Fingerprint of 2026-03-27 taken (server time 2026-11-09T18:09:32.800830Z)

  > Deleted at 2026-11-09T18:10:41.584785Z: 344,061 rows
    table                   rows deleted
    ----------------------  ------------
    option_snapshots        336,480     
    option_chain_snapshots  160         
    delta_snapshots         6,355       
    candle_indicators       482         
    candles                 482         
    candle_sma              7           
    vix_observations        91          
    signals                 4           
  PASS  after the delete, the day no longer matches its fingerprint  (verify FAIL, 03_verify_damaged_sandbox.json)
  PASS  restored instance: every table identical to the fingerprint  (verify PASS, 04_verify_restored_sandbox.json)
  PASS  the database we didn't restore is still damaged  (verify FAIL, 06_verify_live_sandbox_after.json)
    Watch out in the console: enter the restore time in the time zone the field shows (Day 5 lesson).
  [Enter = next step]

==============================================================================
  8. Proof it all works   (~2 min)
  Syllabus topic: consistency checking, end-to-end correctness
==============================================================================
  SAY:
   - Level 1: row counts (sqlite = postgres + rejected). Level 2: per-day sums, min and max.
   - Level 3: the bot's own backtester on SQLite vs on data exported from Postgres, trades diffed.

